> # --- per-cycle wall fusion (wall_fusion.py, lidar_front_wall_node) -----------
> # Step 3 of the wall pipeline: the LiDAR line fits and the stereo column
> # profile (/perception/stereo_columns) are cross-checked column by column and
> # emitted as stateless observations on /perception/wall_observations. The
> # tracker consumes only those. Nothing here remembers anything.
> front_clearance_scene_mask_cloud_stride:
>   default: 4
>   description: "Publish every Nth WALL point on /perception/wall_points. The cloud is for inspection, recording and the stereo-only line fits, NOT for any column decision (those are made on the full-resolution /perception/stereo_columns profile), so decimating it costs nothing real. The full set is ~50k points a frame, about 3 MB/s at 5 Hz straight into every recording."
> front_clearance_scene_mask_column_percentile:
>   default: 20.0
>   description: "Which percentile of a column's WALL depths becomes that column's distance. A LiDAR beam reports its FIRST return, so the honest stereo counterpart is the NEAR end of the column, not its middle: in a column with a near wall low down and a far wall above it the median reports a surface that is not there. A percentile rather than the minimum keeps one bad pixel from deciding the column."
> front_clearance_scene_mask_column_min_px:
>   default: 5
>   description: "Fewest WALL pixels in a column for it to have a stereo distance at all; below this the column reports NaN ('nothing to say'), which is a third state and never a distance."
> lidar_front_wall_stereo_columns_max_age_s:
>   default: 0.15
>   description: "A /perception/stereo_columns profile is paired with a scan only when |t_scan - t_image| is under this [s], measured on the CAMERA IMAGE stamp the message carries, not its arrival. Unpaired, the profile is not used at all: those columns become LIDAR_ONLY or EMPTY and every line is emitted SOURCE_LIDAR, never a stale STEREO_ONLY -- a stereo-only span is the one path that can create a wall out of nothing, and it must never be built from a frame describing a different moment. 0.15 s is bounded by what motion compensation can still fix: at 0.5 m/s it is 7 cm of travel (harmless) but up to 7 deg of yaw at this car's 0.6 m minimum radius, which is a 32-column shift at fx 262 px and IS compensated from odometry (lidar_front_wall_node._compensate)."
> lidar_front_wall_wall_obs_tol_base_m:
>   default: 0.05
>   description: "Constant part of the LiDAR/stereo agreement tolerance [m]: the part that does not come from stereo range noise (extrinsic error, the scan plane vs the column, timing). UNTUNED against a bag with depth."
> lidar_front_wall_wall_obs_tol_quad_per_m2:
>   default: 0.012
>   description: "Range-squared part of the agreement tolerance [m/m^2]: tol = base + this * range^2, because stereo depth error IS quadratic (sigma_Z = Z^2 sigma_d / (fx b); on this rig, ZED 2i with a 0.12 m baseline and fx 262.4 at 640x360, half a pixel of disparity error is 1.6 cm at 1 m and 57 cm at 6 m). A FIXED tolerance would call every far column DISAGREE and every near one AGREE, which says more about the tolerance than about the sensors. 0.012 is ~0.4 px of disparity error."
> lidar_front_wall_wall_obs_disagree_reject_frac:
>   default: 0.5
>   description: "Fraction of a wall's OPINIONATED columns (AGREE + DISAGREE) that must disagree before the whole observation is rejected and counted. Rejection is deliberate: when both sensors report a surface and they are too far apart to be the same one, the honest answer is that this cycle does not know, not that one of them wins. A run of rejections means the extrinsic or the depth is wrong, not that the wall moved, which is why it is published (WallObservationArray.n_rejected) and logged."
> lidar_front_wall_wall_obs_stereo_min_columns:
>   default: 8
>   description: "Fewest CONTIGUOUS stereo-only columns that may become a stereo-sourced wall (glass, or a surface too dark for the LiDAR). One such column is noise: a stray pixel, a reflection edge. This is the one path where nothing cross-checks the result, so the bar is a shape the LiDAR could not plausibly have missed by accident."
> lidar_front_wall_wall_default_height_m:
>   default: 2.5
>   description: "Height [m] above the floor a wall observation is assumed to have when the stereo measured no height for it (a wall outside the camera's view, or one the stereo never confirmed). Heights are only ever taken from the columns the stereo AGREED on; borrowing them for a wall it never saw would invent a measurement. 2.5 m is an indoor corridor wall, and the field is flagged (height_measured false) so a consumer can tell."
> 
> # --- the wall tracker's observation model and FRONT rule (step 4) ------------
> # The tracker consumes ONLY /perception/wall_observations. An observation
> # carries no covariance of its own, so the tracker synthesises one from the
> # two things that determine the error: which sensor saw the wall and how far
> # away it was (multi_wall_tracker.observation_covariance).
> lidar_front_wall_lidar_sigma_base_m:
>   default: 0.02
>   description: "Constant part of a LIDAR observation's distance sigma [m]. The Hokuyo's own range error is millimetres; what this covers is the odometry the line is placed with and the extraction's own bias. Matches the existing meas_sigma_rho_min floor."
> lidar_front_wall_lidar_sigma_per_m:
>   default: 0.005
>   description: "Range-proportional part of a LIDAR observation's sigma [m/m]: the beam footprint grows with range, so a far wall's line is placed slightly less well. LINEAR, not quadratic: unlike stereo, LiDAR range error does not blow up with distance, and at 6 m this is 5 cm against the stereo's 46 cm. That ratio is the honest difference between the sensors and is what makes a LiDAR wall win an argument."
> lidar_front_wall_stereo_sigma_base_m:
>   default: 0.03
>   description: "Constant part of a STEREO observation's distance sigma [m]: the extrinsic and the column summary, before range noise."
> lidar_front_wall_stereo_sigma_quad_per_m2:
>   default: 0.012
>   description: "Range-SQUARED part of a stereo observation's sigma [m/m^2], the same quadratic the cross-check tolerance uses and for the same reason (sigma_Z = Z^2 sigma_d / (fx b)). A wall both sensors agree on gets the inverse-variance combination of the two and is therefore TIGHTER than either alone, which is the whole point of having agreed."
> lidar_front_wall_min_span_for_angle_m:
>   default: 0.3
>   description: "Shortest span [m] used when turning a distance sigma into an angle sigma (sigma_theta = sigma_rho / span). A 5 cm uncertainty on a 3 m wall pins the angle far better than the same 5 cm on a 40 cm stub, and a short segment's angle is exactly what must not be trusted. The floor stops a tiny segment producing an absurd angle sigma."
> lidar_front_wall_stereo_confirm_hits:
>   default: 5
>   description: "Hits within lidar_front_wall_stereo_confirm_window that confirm a track the LiDAR has NEVER seen. Stricter than the ordinary 3-in-5 because stereo-only evidence is the weak kind: on this building's floor the stereo invents whole surfaces out of reflections, and a one-frame reflection must never leave a confirmed wall behind. A LiDAR or BOTH hit promotes the track to the ordinary rule immediately (it is no longer stereo-only); it does NOT confirm it on the spot, so one spurious line still cannot make a wall."
> lidar_front_wall_stereo_confirm_window:
>   default: 7
>   description: "The window the stereo-only confirm rule counts over. Longer than the ordinary 5 so a wall must persist across more of the scene before anything acts on it."
> lidar_front_wall_role_hysteresis_rad:
>   default: 0.0873
>   description: "How far past its 45 deg sector edge [rad, 5 deg] a wall keeps the role it already had. Without it a wall sitting on a boundary flips between front and left every scan as the car wobbles, and every consumer keyed on the role flips with it."
> lidar_front_wall_stereo_front_max_normal_rad:
>   default: 0.5236
>   description: "A STEREO-ONLY wall may be the FRONT wall only when its normal is within this [rad, 30 deg] of the heading. Nothing cross-checks a stereo-only wall, so it may stop the car only when it is squarely across the path, never as a glancing surface. A wall the LiDAR has seen is not subject to this."
> lidar_front_wall_front_strip_half_width_m:
>   default: 0.186
>   description: "Half-width [m] of the strip a track's segment must CROSS to be the FRONT wall, from the front bumper. MUST equal vehicle_frames.front_side_limit_m(). This is the test that separates 'a wall is in the way' from 'a wall's normal points roughly forwards': on an open corridor the side wall satisfies the latter for most of a turn and is nowhere near the car's path, which is how a phantom front wall appeared at 1.1 m (corridor_phantom, 2026-10-15)."
> 
> # --- the front wall output contract (step 5) ---------------------------------
> wall_clear_distance_m:
>   default: 50.0
>   description: "The value the front wall topics carry when the sensors are working and there is NO wall in the way (wall_fusion.STATE_CLEAR). A large FINITE number, and it is not a wall at 50 m: it means 'nothing ahead'. SHARED -- every producer and consumer of a wall distance reads this one key, so 'clear' is the same number everywhere. It must stay far above any mission's stop threshold and any MPC trigger distance, and consumers must CAP or SKIP it rather than feed it into geometry (MPC_corr treats it as unknown). The state this expresses is what the old pipeline could not: with nothing in the strip the LiDAR reported too_few_returns and the stereo plane -- the SIDE wall -- became the front wall at 1.1 m (corridor_phantom, 2026-10-15)."
> 
> # --- wall visualisation and the semantic wall layer (step 7) -----------------
> lidar_front_wall_wall_marker_thickness_m:
>   default: 0.02
>   description: "Thickness [m] of the vertical slab each confirmed wall is drawn as on /perception/walls/markers. A wall is a vertical PLANE, so it is drawn as one rather than as a line on the floor: the slab runs the wall's observed length and stands from z_min to z_max. A wall whose height was only assumed (height_measured false) is drawn fainter, so an assumed 2.5 m is visible at a glance."
